lessons.notes:
## Comparison operators

| Operator | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `===` | equal (value **and** type) |
| `!==` | not equal |
| `<` `>` `<=` `>=` | less / greater |

Avoid `==` and `!=`: they convert types first, so `'0' == 0` is `true`, which surprises people.

## if, else if, else

```javascript
const score = 72;
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log('A');
} else if (score >= 70) {
  console.log('B or C');
} else {
  console.log('Keep practicing');
}
```

Conditions are checked from top to bottom and only the **first** true branch runs.

## Logical operators

- `a && b` is true when both are true.
- `a || b` is true when at least one is true.
- `!a` flips the value.

They "short-circuit": in `a && b`, if `a` is false, `b` is never evaluated.

## Truthy and falsy

Falsy values: `false`, `0`, `-0`, `0n`, `''`, `null`, `undefined`, `NaN`. Everything else (including `'0'`, `[]` and `{}`) is truthy.

## Ternary and switch

```javascript
const label = age >= 18 ? 'adult' : 'minor';

switch (day) {
  case 'sat':
  case 'sun':
    console.log('weekend');
    break;
  default:
    console.log('weekday');
}
```

In a `switch`, do not forget `break`, otherwise execution "falls through" into the next case.

