lessons.notes:
## for loop

```javascript
for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
  console.log(i); // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

`i++` means "add 1 to i". Counting from 0 is common because arrays start at index 0.

## while and do...while

```javascript
let lives = 3;
while (lives > 0) {
  console.log('lives:', lives);
  lives--;
}

let x = 10;
do {
  x++;
} while (x < 5); // body runs once even though the condition is false
```

## for...of and for...in

- `for (const item of array)` gives the **values** of an array or string.
- `for (const key in object)` gives the **keys** of an object. Do not use `for...in` on arrays.

## break and continue

`break` exits the innermost loop. `continue` skips the rest of the current round.

## Building output

Printing many values on one line is easier if you collect them first:

```javascript
const parts = [];
for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) parts.push(i * i);
console.log(parts.join(' ')); // 1 4 9 16 25
```

## Watch out for infinite loops

If the condition never becomes false, the program never ends. Averroes Code lets you stop a running program, but it is better to check that your loop variable moves toward the end condition.

## Nested loops

A loop inside a loop runs the inner loop completely for each round of the outer loop, which is useful for grids and tables.

