lessons.notes:
## Defining a class

```javascript
class Rectangle {
  constructor(width, height) {
    this.width = width;
    this.height = height;
  }
  area() {
    return this.width * this.height;
  }
}
const r = new Rectangle(3, 4);
r.area(); // 12
```

- The `constructor` runs once when you call `new`.
- `this` is the new object.
- Class names use **PascalCase**.

## Fields, getters and setters

```javascript
class Temperature {
  #celsius = 0;                      // private field
  get fahrenheit() { return this.#celsius * 9 / 5 + 32; }
  set celsius(v) {
    if (v < -273.15) throw new Error('Too cold');
    this.#celsius = v;
  }
}
```

Private `#fields` cannot be read from outside the class, which protects the object's rules.

## Inheritance

```javascript
class Shape { describe() { return `Area: ${this.area()}`; } }
class Square extends Shape {
  constructor(side) {
    super();          // must call super() before using this
    this.side = side;
  }
  area() { return this.side ** 2; }
}
```

Use `instanceof` to check a type: `new Square(2) instanceof Shape` is `true`.

## Static members

`static` methods and fields live on the class: `Rectangle.fromSquare(5)`.

## Behind the scenes

Classes are built on JavaScript's **prototypes**: methods are shared by all instances through `Rectangle.prototype`. You rarely need to touch prototypes directly, but it explains why methods are not copied into every object.

## Composition over inheritance

Deep inheritance trees get hard to change. Often it is simpler for an object to **have** helpers than to **be** a subtype.

lessons.example:
class Shape {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  describe() { return `${this.name} with area ${this.area().toFixed(2)}`; }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
  constructor(r) { super('Circle'); this.r = r; }
  area() { return Math.PI * this.r ** 2; }
}
class Rect extends Shape {
  constructor(w, h) { super('Rectangle'); this.w = w; this.h = h; }
  area() { return this.w * this.h; }
}
for (const s of [new Circle(1), new Rect(2, 3)]) {
  console.log(s.describe());
}

