lessons.notes:
## Kinds of errors

| Type | Example |
|---|---|
| `SyntaxError` | missing bracket, invalid JSON |
| `ReferenceError` | using a variable that does not exist |
| `TypeError` | calling something that is not a function, reading a property of `null` |
| `RangeError` | invalid array length, too much recursion |

Read error messages carefully: they include the type, a message, and a stack trace with line numbers.

## try / catch / finally

```javascript
try {
  const data = JSON.parse(text);
  console.log(data.name);
} catch (err) {
  console.log(`Bad input: ${err.message}`);
} finally {
  console.log('done');
}
```

If you do not need the error object, you can write `catch { ... }`.

## Throwing

```javascript
function setAge(age) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(age) || age < 0) {
    throw new RangeError(`Invalid age: ${age}`);
  }
  return age;
}
```

Always throw `Error` objects (or subclasses), not plain strings, so you get a stack trace.

## Custom errors and instanceof

```javascript
class NotFoundError extends Error {
  constructor(what) { super(`${what} not found`); this.name = 'NotFoundError'; }
}
try { ... } catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof NotFoundError) { /* handle */ }
  else throw e; // re-throw errors you do not understand
}
```

## Good practice

- Validate input early and throw clear messages.
- Catch errors where you can actually do something useful about them.
- Do not silently swallow errors with an empty `catch {}`.
- `Number('abc')` does not throw; it gives `NaN`. Check with `Number.isNaN(x)`.

