lessons.notes:
## Why asynchronous?

Reading a file, waiting for a timer or downloading data can take a long time. If JavaScript waited, the whole page or program would freeze. Instead it **starts** the task and handles the result later.

## The event loop in one picture

1. Your code runs to the end.
2. Finished tasks put their callbacks in a queue.
3. The event loop takes callbacks from the queue one by one.

Promise callbacks (microtasks) run before timer callbacks, so `Promise.resolve().then(...)` runs before `setTimeout(..., 0)`.

## Promises

```javascript
function delay(ms, value) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (ms < 0) reject(new Error('negative delay'));
    else setTimeout(() => resolve(value), ms);
  });
}

delay(100, 'hi')
  .then((v) => v.toUpperCase())
  .then((v) => console.log(v))   // HI
  .catch((e) => console.log(e.message));
```

## async / await

```javascript
async function run() {
  try {
    const v = await delay(100, 'hello');
    console.log(v);
  } catch (e) {
    console.log('Error:', e.message);
  }
}
run();
```

- `await` only works inside `async` functions (and at the top level of ES modules).
- An `async` function always returns a Promise.

## Running tasks together

| Helper | Resolves when |
|---|---|
| `Promise.all(list)` | all succeed (rejects on first failure) |
| `Promise.allSettled(list)` | all finish, success or not |
| `Promise.race(list)` | the first one settles |
| `Promise.any(list)` | the first one succeeds |

## Common mistakes

- Forgetting `await`: you get a pending Promise instead of the value.
- `await` inside a `forEach` does not wait; use `for...of` or `Promise.all`.
- Unhandled rejections crash Node.js programs: always `catch`.

